  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "Mutant execution is underspecified: changing the module may not affect ModelView wrappers, and count-minus-one readout occurs outside the convolution backbone. Each fault needs an explicit execution path and activation check.",
    "Dilation changes may remain entirely inside the clean receptive field, making outside-field invariance insufficient for detection. If the clean receptive field covers all occupied history positions, that probe has zero eligible comparisons.",
    "Calibration needs identity-wrapper controls, repeated clean executions, prespecified mutation sites and severity levels, and separate counts for activated, behaviorally observable, and detected faults. Three handpicked mutants cannot establish broad detection reliability.",
    "A state-dictionary hash does not detect changes to dilation, padding, hooks, or other Python attributes. Cleanup must verify these attributes and reproduce clean outputs after every mutant, including exception paths.",
    "Originality 4 is reasonable, but potential 7 remains speculative and difficulty 3 appears optimistic. Exact convolution semantics, independent reference checks, wrapper integration, and restoration require substantial care. A forward-pass budget is also missing, particularly if existing expensive audits run again.",
    "Mutation calibration is a debugging contribution; it does not directly address the reported recommendation failures or establish improved validation HR@10. Qualitative intent claims remain unsupported without the underlying recommendation and feedback records."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Determine how to construct observable, fault-specific tests when receptive-field invariance cannot distinguish a dilation mutant.",
      "query": "NextItNet causal dilated residual convolution mutation testing: how can an independent impulse-response or layerwise reference verify dilation and asymmetric padding when the clean receptive field covers the entire input sequence? Include residual branches, normalization axes, masking, and shape-preserving padding changes."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Strengthen calibration against circular tests, equivalent mutants, and selection of easily detected faults.",
      "query": "Neural network mutation testing equivalent mutants fault observability mutation score calibration: recommended identity controls, mutation-site coverage, severity sweeps, clean numerical baselines, and held-out mutation operators for inference-only CNN testing with frozen trained weights."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Resolve implementation and cleanup details without fitting or deploying additional models.",
      "query": "PyTorch temporary inference fault injection Conv1d dilation padding forward hooks torch.func.functional_call: how to ensure wrappers execute the mutated path and restore module attributes, hooks, buffers, RNG states, training flags, and clean outputs after exceptions? Explain why state_dict equality alone is insufficient."
    }
  ]
}
